  "body": "# Associated User Story\r\nAs a manager, I want to have different types of points for attendance, and have a certain number of each point type determine member status rather than total points.\r\n\r\nAs an officer, I don’t have to be tracked in the point dashboard because there is no point requirement for me.\r\n\r\n# Acceptance Criteria\r\n### Manager can define point types and set a minimum required for each type, and member status is based on meeting those minimums instead of total points.\r\n- Sunny: A manager opens the point settings and sees the available point types (e.g., service, personal development, social). They set a minimum for each type (e.g., 3 service, 2 personal development, 2 social) and save. When a member attends an event, the points are added to the correct type. A member who meets every minimum shows as \"active.\" A member who has enough total points but is below the minimum in any single type shows as \"inactive,\" with the missing requirement noted (e.g., \"needs 1 more service point\").\r\n-Rainy: A manager tries to save a point requirement that is invalid (a blank field, a negative number, or a non-number), the settings fail to save, or a regular member tries to change the requirements. The system shows a clear error message (e.g., \"Enter a whole number of 0 or more\" or \"Changes could not be saved, try again or contact an admin\") instead of failing silently or saving bad values. The previous requirements stay in place so member statuses aren't accidentally changed, and only officers can edit the settings.\r\n###Officers are excluded from point tracking and active/inactive status, and they do not appear as members who need to meet point requirements.\r\n-Sunny: A member is promoted to officer. Their name no longer appears in the point dashboard, and they are not counted toward any active or inactive totals. They are never shown as \"inactive\" or flagged for missing points, even if they have no points or are below the minimums. Officers can still view the dashboard to see regular members' points and statuses. If an officer is later changed back to a regular member, they reappear in the dashboard and are tracked from that point on, with any points they had already earned still counted.\r\n- Rainy: The system can't tell whether someone is an officer (for example, the role fails to load or the role data is missing), or an officer's role is changed and the dashboard doesn't update. The system shows a clear message (e.g., \"We couldn't confirm this member's role, try again or contact an admin\") instead of wrongly marking an officer as inactive or hiding a regular member from tracking. When the role status is uncertain, the member's existing status stays unchanged until the role can be confirmed, so no one is incorrectly flagged or dropped from the dashboard.\r\n\r\nDefinition of Done -\r\nA story is done when its acceptance criteria are met and every item in the team PR template is satisfied:\r\n(A) code review by a non-author, coding standard met, RuboCop checked with output/CI link, and setup changes documented;\r\n(B) the human-only maintainability checks (meaningful comments and names, skinny controllers, thin views, DRY, Rails conventions, foreign keys, enums, nested routes);\r\n(C) tests added or updated and passing. Any deferred items or RuboCop exclusions are explained in Section D of the PR.",
